  "answer_text": "## What the log is telling you\n\nThe program intentionally passes a garbage pointer as the destination `cpumask`:\n\n```c\nstruct bpf_cpumask *invalid = (struct bpf_cpumask *)0x123456;\nret = bpf_cpumask_populate((struct cpumask *)invalid, &bits, sizeof(bits));\n```\n\nSo at the call site `R1` holds the scalar constant `0x123456`, and the verifier\ncorrectly rejects it:\n\n```\n4: (85) call bpf_cpumask_populate#61745\nR1 type=scalar expected=fp\n```\n\nThis is **not** a bug in the program logic \u2014 passing a bogus pointer here *must*\nfail verification. The program is a *negative* (`__failure`) test.\n\n## Why the test is \"failing\"\n\nThe source is missing the test-harness annotations that tell `test_loader` that\nthis program is **expected** to fail verification (and with which message).\nWithout `__failure`/`__msg`, the loader assumes the program should load\nsuccessfully, so the (correct) verifier rejection is reported as a test failure.\n\n## The fix\n\nAdd the `bpf_misc.h` annotations marking the program as an expected failure, and\nmatch the verifier message shown in the log.\n\n```c\n#include <vmlinux.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_tracing.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n#include \"bpf_misc.h\"\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n\nSEC(\"tp_btf/task_newtask\")\n__failure __msg(\"type=scalar expected=fp\")\nint BPF_PROG(entry, struct task_struct *task, u64 flags)\n{\n\tstruct bpf_cpumask *invalid = (struct bpf_cpumask *)0x123456;\n\tu64 bits;\n\tint ret;\n\n\tret = bpf_cpumask_populate((struct cpumask *)invalid, &bits, sizeof(bits));\n\tif (!ret)\n\t\treturn 2;\n\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe only source-level edits are:\n1. `#include \"bpf_misc.h\"` (provides the `__failure`/`__msg` macros).\n2. `__failure __msg(\"type=scalar expected=fp\")` on the program.\n\nWith these, the harness now expects the verifier to reject the program and\nchecks that the rejection message contains `type=scalar expected=fp`, which\nmatches the log exactly, so the test passes.\n\n(If, instead, the intent were for the program to genuinely load, you would have\nto pass a real `bpf_cpumask` obtained from `bpf_cpumask_create()` rather than the\nfabricated `0x123456` pointer \u2014 but the `invalid` naming makes clear this is\nmeant to be a failure test.)",
